// The number scanner, exercised through `parse` -- `P` and `scan_number` are
// private to `std/json`, so a fixture has no other route to them.
//
// Each accepted case prints a label and what `stringify` produces from the
// parsed value, so the golden pins the whole path at once: the span the scanner
// matched, the `Float` the intrinsic produced from that span, and the
// rendering. The expected renderings were derived from JSON's grammar and
// Rust's `f64` Display, not captured from this program's output -- `1e3` is one
// thousand and renders `1000`, `1.5E-2` is fifteen thousandths and renders
// `0.015`, and `-0` keeps its sign because an f64 negative zero is a distinct
// bit pattern that Display shows.
//
// `report` takes a LABEL as well as the source text because two inputs below
// cannot be echoed: leading and trailing spaces are invisible in a golden, and
// a form feed would put a raw control byte into the `.stdout` file. For every
// other case the label is the source text, so those lines read as before.
//
// Each rejected case prints the message and the character index. `01`, `+1`,
// `.5` and the form feed each produce TWO failures -- the first from the value
// walk, then `parse`'s trailing-content check on the remainder nothing consumed
// -- so those FOUR goldens are this fixture's share of `fail`'s
// first-failure-wins guard: without it each printed message would be the
// trailing-content one. Counted, not eyeballed: dropping the guard changes
// exactly those four lines here (and 20 more across the strings and values
// fixtures). `1e`, `1.` and `-` consume to the end of the input and so produce
// exactly one failure each.
//
// `+1` and `.5` are rejected by `parse_value`'s DISPATCH, not by the number
// scanner: neither `+` nor `.` can begin a JSON value, so neither reaches
// `scan_number` at all and both report `expected a value`. `-` is the case that
// still reaches the scanner's own `expected a digit` branch, since `-` is the
// one sign JSON allows and so is dispatched here before the digit is missing.
//
// The last two cases are this fixture's share of `skip_ws`'s coverage, in both
// directions -- NOT the whole of it, and `' 1 '` is NOT the only input whose
// leading whitespace must be SKIPPED. `json_parse_values.nova` carries three
// whitespace cases of its own, one of them filling all twelve `ws` slots RFC
// 8259's structural rules define.
//
// Measured rather than divided up by eye: narrowing the predicate to drop
// `c == ' '` fails TWO targets, changing this fixture's one line -- `' 1 '`
// becomes `expected a value at 0` -- and SEVEN of that one's. So the values
// fixture is the stronger half of this coverage, not a duplicate of it.
//
// The form feed is the other direction: RFC 8259 admits exactly space, tab, line
// feed and carriage return as whitespace, so a form feed must NOT be skipped,
// and widening the predicate to admit it turns this line from an error into `1`.
fn main() {
    report("0", "0")
    report("-0", "-0")
    report("12", "12")
    report("-3.5", "-3.5")
    report("1e3", "1e3")
    report("1.5E-2", "1.5E-2")
    report("2.5", "2.5")

    report("01", "01")
    report("+1", "+1")
    report(".5", ".5")
    report("1e", "1e")
    report("1.", "1.")
    report("-", "-")

    report("' 1 '", " 1 ")
    report("'\\f1'", "\u{0C}1")
}

fn report(label: String, src: String) {
    match parse(src) {
        Ok(v) => println("${label} -> ${stringify(v)}")
        Err(e) => println("${label} -> error: ${e.msg} at ${e.at}")
    }
}
